  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Role and Authority
    
    Shadow-Broker Nyla was the unofficial mayor of Nightfall Shelf, the labyrinthine tunnel-colony carved into the Berg's shadowed interior. She held no elected office. Her authority derived from leadership of the Midnight Claws, the syndicate that controlled the Shelf's shadow economy — smuggling, enforcement, fire-core diversion, and the quiet disappearances that the Fire Keepers preferred not to acknowledge. For thirty winters, Nyla's word carried more weight than any council vote in the deep tunnels. The Fire Keepers tolerated her because the alternative was tunnel-war and resource collapse; she tolerated the Fire Keepers because someone had to maintain the volcanic cores, and the Keepers were too rigid to manage the gray spaces where survival actually happened.
    
    Her informant network extended upward into Aurora Stack's crystal towers despite the Flipper Accord's careful silences between the two colonies. During orca incursions, those same informants tracked raiding pod approaches through the tunnels, providing the Midnight Claws with warning at three junctions' distance — a defensive function never formally recognized and therefore never formally compensated.
    
    ## Rise to Power
    
    Nyla inherited the Midnight Claws at nineteen by murdering her predecessor. The tunnel-chants frame this as necessity: the fires were dying and the previous Shadow-Broker would not descend deep enough to restart them. Whether this is justification or honest assessment depends on which tradition one consults. I note, because the official histories have been quietly amended to obscure it, that the predecessor in question was Aurora-born, not Nightshelf — a detail I recovered from administrative ice in the Foundation Depths. The Shadow-Broker title was not always anchored to a single culture, whatever the current orthodoxy implies.
    
    Nyla's left flipper bore forge-scars she refused to discuss. The skin was mottled and rigid — useful for gripping but painful in cold. She favored it deliberately. The Berg's creation scripture attributes the scarring to her drawing the first molten fire-core from the deep veins with bare flippers and carving the first law-glyphs into the tunnel walls. The prosaic explanation — that the work of managing volcanic infrastructure leaves marks — is less frequently repeated.
    
    ## Operations and Associations
    
    Nyla trained Augur ☽'Silvsiaelrous~ the Resolute, the crystalline-weaver and orca negotiator, in the ☽'glacial Crystalline~ Spiral during the Augur's apprenticeship. The Spiral — a Nightshelf technique that fractures time and memory within ice, discovered by Order founder Umbra-Princeps Alnae~ Oss — is dangerous enough that its deployment leaves recursive ice-fracture patterns in the surrounding structure. Nyla reportedly used the technique at or near the Glow-Fissure, the deep corruption source-point, prior to her confession. The fracture patterns remain visible at the second descent level. The Shadow-Walk Detachment's subsequent reconnaissance assessed these patterns as hazardous, which is a polite way of saying they could not determine what the technique had done or whether it was still doing it.
    
    She was never seen in aurora-light and actively avoided it. She drank fire-tea at odd hours, alone. She had not visited the memorial shrines in three years before her confession.
    
    ## The Confession
    
    Nyla climbed to Aurora Stack and spoke for three hours in the Lens-Cathedral under clear skies, laying out thirty winters of the Midnight Claws' operations with ledger precision: smuggling routes, fire-core diversions, the murder of her predecessor. She framed the thirty winters as custodianship indistinguishable from corruption — the last in a line stretching back to the first shaper who burrowed into the god's flesh.
    
    She mentioned the killing of ☽'Ilun the Refracted, an ice-reader who had studied the Thaw-Paths Medallion, exactly once, dismissing it as "a complication resolved" before moving to the next item. The summary is canonical: Nyla killed ☽'Ilun to hide crimes the Medallion had revealed.
    
    What the confession omitted is as significant as what it contained. Auditor Yunjoushong's subsequent investigation established that the Thaw-Paths Medallion revealed a second map showing attack vectors through the Stack's eastern ridge, and evidence that Council member Amaang Skael'Compass had deliberately dismantled defenses before the Shattered-Spire Assault, the orca attack that devastated the eastern galleries. Nyla confessed to everything the aurora could judge while burying what the Medallion had revealed about sabotage from within Aurora Stack's own governance. The confession was real, verified, and complete — in exactly the way a sealed crate is complete when no one checks the false bottom.
    
    ## Aftermath
    
    Nyla vanished into the deep tunnels immediately after her confession. The Shadow Parliament, the Nightshelf's judicial body, brought formal charges against the Midnight Claws under the dissolution statute Iudicium∴culum. The organization was dissolved, its members sentenced to supervised labor-service. Nyla herself was beyond reach.
    
    She was later confirmed dead, though the circumstances of her death were never established. Her informant network persisted as a diminished remnant, accessible to those who knew the right channels. The command vacuum she created left the Glow-Fissure's defenses to Fire Keeper personnel untrained for that duty — a transfer of responsibility that the Shadow-Walk Detachment's assessment would later identify as a critical vulnerability. The specific door she had guarded has no guardian. The seal she maintained holds by habit rather than force.
    
    ## Scriptural Tradition
    
    In the Berg's creation scripture — compiled from the Nightshelf Recitation and the Aurora Witness — Nyla is venerated as *She Who Kept*, one of three primordial figures who shaped the world alongside High-Beak Auditor Selka of Aurora Stack and Grandmother Deep, the ancient entity worshipped by the orca Wake-Singers. The scripture names Nyla the Fire-Keeper and the Hands-in-the-Dark: the one who drew heat from the Berg's volcanic heart and distributed it through every corridor, who sealed primordial doors behind which something still knocks.
    
    When the three primordial figures discovered the Omega Obelisk — a whale-bone artifact older than all of them — Nyla pressed her scarred flipper to its surface and pulled back. It is the only time in any tradition she is recorded as retreating from anything. As their cooperation fractured, she rationed fire to maintain the ancient seals, choosing which tunnels stayed warm and which cooled — the first selective distribution of warmth in the deep.
    
    The two traditions disagree on whether her turning from the aurora-light was failure or salvation. Both acknowledge that without the fire she kept, the ice would have swallowed every living thing on the Berg's surface.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771575496764_0", anchor: "For thirty winters, Nyla's word carried more weight than any council vote", text: "Thirty winters. Round. I flagged in my Glow-Fissure temporal annotation that Midnight Claws administrative records in the deep ice predate any Thaw-era date marker I can identify. 'Thirty winters' is Nyla's own number — the span she confessed to, coterminous with her personal reign. She was accounting for her tenure, not the organization's operational history. The institution she inherited was already functioning before the dates she admitted to. The ledger she opened for the Aurora council had a start date, and that start date was a choice.", type: :skepticism, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771575496764_1", anchor: "The confession was real, verified, and complete — in exactly the way a sealed crate is complete when no one checks the false bottom", text: "How extraordinary that she did this. She climbed into aurora-light she had avoided her entire life, spoke for three hours with ledger precision, and the one thing she buried was evidence that Aurora Stack's own governance had enabled the worst orca attack in recent memory. She gave them everything they could use against her and withheld everything they could use against themselves. I have been puzzling at this for longer than the entry warranted. It is the most precisely constructed act of honesty I have encountered in the archive. She understood — as very few do — that a confession can be a weapon if the confessor chooses what is missing.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771575496764_2", anchor: "She was later confirmed dead, though the circumstances of her death were never established", text: "The Nightshelf's foremost expert in quiet disappearances quietly disappeared. I find this less than conclusive.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771575496764_3", anchor: "Nyla trained Augur ☽'Silvsiaelrous~ the Resolute", text: "The apprenticeship records I recovered from the administrative ice list both names, but the indexing conventions of that stratum are ambiguous about directionality. It is equally consistent with the Augur — already a gifted crystalline-weaver — having instructed Nyla in the Spiral's finer applications. The Augur's own entry credits her with a gift for reading frozen memories that preceded any known teacher. I wrote the main text with the conventional reading. The ice permits the reverse.", type: :correction, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771575496764_4", anchor: "a whale-bone artifact older than all of them", text: "Whale-bone. The Wake-Singers worship a dead god whose corpse drifts in the deep — a god that, by every account, possessed bones. An artifact carved from that material, older than all three primordial figures, sits at the center of the creation scripture, and no tradition from either colony addresses the obvious question of whose bone it was. Nyla pressed her scarred flipper to its surface and pulled back. The only retreat in her entire mythos. Remarkable that no one asks what she felt.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771575496764_5", anchor: "the first selective distribution of warmth in the deep", text: "This is the mechanism the Flipper Accord later formalized — fire-core allocation as governance. The secret drafts I annotated in the Fissure☽ Aurora entry contain provisions written in language that echoes the scriptural phrasing almost exactly: 'choosing which tunnels stayed warm.' Either the Accord's drafters were consciously quoting the Recitation, or the scripture was back-written from the Accord's administrative language and projected onto a primordial figure to make rationing sound like theology. Given what I know about how institutions commission retrospectives, I have my suspicion.", type: :commentary, display: :full
